# Formatting (TS/JS)

**Agent check (every new/edited signature, dep array, destructure, or import):** one line when it
fits 150 chars; when wrapping, **pack** multiple names per line. **Never** one name per line. ESLint
autofixes imports only — params, deps and destructures are on you.

## Max line length

- **150 characters, every repo.** One number — there is no per-repo ceiling any more.
- Enforce with `@stylistic/max-len` when the repo has ESLint wired.

## Pack, don't stack — one rule, four places

Applies to **named imports**, **hook dependency arrays**, **function / component parameter lists**, and **destructuring assignments** (`const { … } = useThing()`):

- Keep the list on **one line** when it fits the 150-char budget. This is the common case.
- When wrapping is required, **fill each line** with as many entries as fit — **do not** put one entry per line (Prettier-style).
- Your untrained default is Prettier-at-80, so you will drift to one-per-line in all four unless you check. Check.
- After a repo adopts import lint, **params, hook deps and destructures are where one-per-line leaks back** — nothing autofixes them.

**Exception — JSX props stay one per line.** A JSX attribute is a `name={expression}` pair, often a multi-line arrow; packing those reads worse. This rule is about *identifier lists*, not JSX. Interface/type members also keep one per line.

### Named imports

- Wrap in the **brace-newline form**: `{` ends the `import` line, names follow packed on 2-space-indented lines, `} from "…";` closes on its own line.
- **Keep the trailing comma** — the closing brace is on its own line.
- Where names have a natural grouping, give each group its own line. That is why this form wins.

```ts
// GOOD — single line when it fits
import { useCallback, useEffect, useMemo, useRef } from "react";

// GOOD — brace-newline, packed, grouped by meaning
import {
  Background, BackgroundVariant, MarkerType, ReactFlow,
  useEdgesState, useNodesState, type Edge, type Node,
} from "@xyflow/react";

// BAD — the only form the rule flags
import {
  useCallback,
  useEffect,
  useMemo,
  useRef,
} from "react";
```

### Hook dependency arrays

```ts
// GOOD — one line
}, [showSidePanel, mood, analyzing, imageCount, uploading, projectId]);

// GOOD — wrapped and packed when 20 deps will not fit in 150 chars
}, [
  showSidePanel, mood, analyzing, imageCount, uploading, projectId, savingBrief, briefSaved,
  fragments, savedPrompts, activeSessionId, handleAddPromptToShotlist, handleDeletePrompt,
]);

// BAD — one dependency per line
}, [
  showSidePanel,
  mood,
  analyzing,
]);
```

A dep array needing three packed lines is a **design signal** — the memo is probably doing several jobs and wants splitting.

### Function & component parameter lists

```ts
// GOOD — fits on one line, so it stays there
function ImagesPage({ projectId, onBack, onSelectProducts, onNeedProject, returnToShotlist = false }: ImagesPageProps) {

// GOOD — wrapped and packed, trailing comma kept
const AddNodeButton = ({
  disabled, i, nodeType, nodeDisplayName, onNodeCreateClick, tileId, onActivate,
}: IAddNodeButtonProps) => {

// GOOD — many destructured props (15+), still packed — not one name per line
export function ConsoleButton({
  label, subLabel, variant = "idle", className, onClick, sfx, fieldView, fieldCamera,
  borderOuter, borderInner, showBackground = true, showBorderOuter = true, showBorderInner = true,
  behavior, pinBorderInner = false, pinBorderOuter = false,
}: ConsoleButtonProps) {

// BAD — one parameter per line (Prettier default; what you write unprompted)
function ImagesPage({
  projectId,
  onBack,
  onSelectProducts,
}: ImagesPageProps) {
```

- **Do not add `@stylistic/object-curly-newline`.** With `ImportDeclaration: 'never'` it forbids
  the break straight after `{` — which is exactly the form above — so it would make the house
  style unlintable. It was removed from the standard deliberately.

### Destructuring assignments

A `const { … } = useSomething()` is an identifier list like any other. It is the position this
rule missed longest, because it reads as *calling a hook* rather than as a list — but the shape
on the page is identical to a parameter list, and so is the fix.

```ts
// GOOD — one line when it fits
const { data, isLoading, isError, error } = useAccountSummary();

// GOOD — wrapped and packed when the names will not fit 150 chars
const {
  accounts, spentToday, spentThisMonth, discretionaryThisMonth,
  discretionaryBudget, isLoading, isError, error,
} = useAccountSummary();

// BAD — one name per line (Prettier default; what you write unprompted)
const {
  accounts,
  spentToday,
  spentThisMonth,
} = useAccountSummary();
```

**Enforcement:** the ESLint rule covers **imports only**. Copy
`projects/coding-standards/eslint-rules/packed-named-imports.mjs` from the hub into the
consuming repo's `eslint-rules/` and wire it at `maxLineLength: 150`. **Hook deps, parameter
lists and destructuring assignments are not linted** — they are on you and on review. A clean
`eslint` run proves nothing about them.
